-- Cases for the seat questions in sgbau-seat.l4.  Expected values written by hand from the sources,
-- from the table in NOTES.md, not copied from the encoding's own output.
--
-- FORWARD-LOOKING ROW, TARGET unstable.
-- Every case passes both dates, so no case reads the wall clock.
--
-- The seat questions take the facts positionally, in the seat's own order:
--   conduct day, reading day,
--   the kind of receiver (television, radio, satellite dish),
--   pictures received, sound received, for anyone with equipment,
--   installed, imported or sold, operates or has on premises,
--   (held a licence -- questions 2 and 3 only)

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sgbau-nouns`
IMPORT `sgbau-seat`

TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

§ `Four people, and what each has`

-- A television owner: a set used to see and hear broadcasts, pictures and sound received from programmes sent
-- to anyone with a set, kept at home and watched (operated), not installed, not sold.
GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a television owner needs a licence` MEANS
    `Did I need a licence for my apparatus` conduct reading
        --  kind        pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper
            television  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE

-- A radio listener: a set used to hear broadcasts, sound only.
GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a radio listener needs a licence` MEANS
    `Did I need a licence for my apparatus` conduct reading
        --  kind   pic    snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper
            radio  FALSE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE

-- A dish owner: a satellite dish system, programmes with pictures and sound.
GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a dish owner needs a licence` MEANS
    `Did I need a licence for my apparatus` conduct reading
        --  kind              pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper
            `satellite dish`  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE

-- A television owner whose set receives nothing (not tuned to any service).
GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a television owner with nothing received needs a licence` MEANS
    `Did I need a licence for my apparatus` conduct reading
        --  kind        pic    snd    anyone  inst   sold   oper
            television  FALSE  FALSE  FALSE   FALSE  FALSE  TRUE

§ `1. Did I need a licence?  (conduct on a day, read on a day)`

-- Conduct 1 June 2011.  Before 31 October 2012 the three-item Schedule governs for every reader.
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `a radio listener needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `a dish owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- Read on 31 October 2012 or later, the substituted Schedule governs 2011 conduct: only the dish.
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 31 10 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a radio listener needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 31 10 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a dish owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 31 10 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a radio listener needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a dish owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS TRUE
-- Conduct 31 December 2010: S 549/2012 is deemed to operate only from 1 January 2011.
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `a radio listener needs a licence` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- Conduct 1 January 2011, the first day S 549/2012 is deemed to operate.
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- Conduct today (3 November 2026, pinned): a television needs no licence; a dish does.
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 3 11 2026) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a dish owner needs a licence` (Date 3 11 2026) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS TRUE
-- Conduct today, law read on 1 August 2012: the law as it then read, applied to a day still to come.
#ASSERT `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 3 11 2026) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- A set that receives nothing needs no licence under s 20(1)(c) on any day.
#ASSERT `a television owner with nothing received needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a television owner with nothing received needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS FALSE
-- Outside the sources: before the first deposited edition (31 July 2003).
#ASSERT REFUSED `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 1 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)
#ASSERT REFUSED `a television owner with nothing received needs a licence` (Date 1 1 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)
#ASSERT REFUSED `a dish owner needs a licence` (Date 30 7 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)
-- A reader before the first deposited edition.
#ASSERT REFUSED `a television owner needs a licence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 30 7 2003)

§ `2 and 3. If I had no licence: an offence, and the most I could be fined`

-- The television owner, no licence, conduct 1 June 2011.
GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an unlicensed television owner commits an offence` MEANS
    `Would the apparatus without a licence have been an offence` conduct reading
        --  kind        pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper  licensed
            television  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE  FALSE

GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a licensed television owner commits an offence` MEANS
    `Would the apparatus without a licence have been an offence` conduct reading
        --  kind        pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper  licensed
            television  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE  TRUE

GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most an unlicensed television owner could be fined` MEANS
    `What is the most I could have been fined` conduct reading
        --  kind        pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper  licensed
            television  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE  FALSE

GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most an unlicensed dish owner could be fined` MEANS
    `What is the most I could have been fined` conduct reading
        --  kind              pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper  licensed
            `satellite dish`  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE  FALSE

-- Read on 1 August 2012, 2011 conduct: the Act then listed televisions, so an offence, fine up to $40,000.
#ASSERT `an unlicensed television owner commits an offence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed television owner could be fined` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS 40000
-- Read on 1 November 2012, the same 2011 conduct is no offence: the retrospective Notification removed it.
#ASSERT `an unlicensed television owner commits an offence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed television owner could be fined` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS 0
-- 2010 conduct stays an offence for every reader.
#ASSERT `an unlicensed television owner commits an offence` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed television owner could be fined` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS 40000
-- A licence under s 20 means there is no contravention.
#ASSERT `a licensed television owner commits an offence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS FALSE
-- The dish is still caught, under both texts.
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed dish owner could be fined` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS 40000
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed dish owner could be fined` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS 40000
-- Outside the sources.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the most an unlicensed television owner could be fined` (Date 1 1 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)

§ `3. Which text of the First Schedule applied to me?`

#ASSERT `Which text of the First Schedule applied` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS `the First Schedule of three items`
#ASSERT `Which text of the First Schedule applied` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS `the First Schedule of three items`
#ASSERT `Which text of the First Schedule applied` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 31 10 2012) EQUALS `the First Schedule of the TVRO system only`
#ASSERT `Which text of the First Schedule applied` (Date 3 11 2026) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS `the First Schedule of the TVRO system only`
#ASSERT REFUSED `Which text of the First Schedule applied` (Date 30 7 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)

§ `4. Over a period`

GIVEN first IS A DATE, last IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` MEANS
    `Did I need a licence on any day in the period` first last reading
        --  kind        pic   snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper
            television  TRUE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE

-- 1 Dec 2010 to 31 Jan 2011, read on 1 August 2012: every day under the three-item text.
#ASSERT `a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 12 2010) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- The same period read on 1 November 2012: December 2010 days are still under the three-item text.
#ASSERT `a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 12 2010) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- January 2011 alone, read on 1 November 2012: every day under the TVRO-only text.
#ASSERT `a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS FALSE
-- January 2011 alone, read on 1 August 2012.
#ASSERT `a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- A period wholly after 2012 read today.
#ASSERT `a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 1 2013) (Date 31 12 2013) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS FALSE
-- A period that reaches back before the first deposited edition refuses.
#ASSERT REFUSED `a television owner needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 7 2003) (Date 31 8 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)

§ `The radio, at the same days as the television`

-- The First Schedule's item 2, "Broadcast sound receiver", is in the three-item text and gone from the TVRO-only text.
-- A radio set is treated as the Act treats it: AM and FM are not told apart.
GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an unlicensed radio listener commits an offence` MEANS
    `Would the apparatus without a licence have been an offence` conduct reading
        --  kind   pic    snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper  licensed
            radio  FALSE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE  FALSE

GIVEN conduct IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most an unlicensed radio listener could be fined` MEANS
    `What is the most I could have been fined` conduct reading
        --  kind   pic    snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper  licensed
            radio  FALSE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE  FALSE

GIVEN first IS A DATE, last IS A DATE, reading IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a radio listener needed a licence on some day of the period` MEANS
    `Did I need a licence on any day in the period` first last reading
        --  kind   pic    snd   anyone  inst   sold   oper
            radio  FALSE  TRUE  TRUE    FALSE  FALSE  TRUE

-- Read on 1 August 2012, 2011 conduct: the Act then listed sound receivers, so an offence, fine up to $40,000.
#ASSERT `an unlicensed radio listener commits an offence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed radio listener could be fined` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS 40000
-- Read on 1 November 2012, the same 2011 conduct is no offence.
#ASSERT `an unlicensed radio listener commits an offence` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed radio listener could be fined` (Date 1 6 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS 0
-- Conduct on 31 December 2010 stays an offence for every reader; conduct today is not an offence.
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed radio listener could be fined` (Date 31 12 2010) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS 40000
#ASSERT `the most an unlicensed radio listener could be fined` (Date 3 11 2026) (Date 3 11 2026) EQUALS 0
-- The period: the radio follows the television across 1 January 2011.
#ASSERT `a radio listener needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 12 2010) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `a radio listener needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 11 2012) EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT `a radio listener needed a licence on some day of the period` (Date 1 1 2011) (Date 31 1 2011) (Date 1 8 2012) EQUALS TRUE
-- Outside the sources.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the most an unlicensed radio listener could be fined` (Date 1 1 2003) (Date 3 11 2026)
